<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>$(DEVELOPMENT_LANGUAGE)</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Rotelyx Chat</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<!-- Live Activities. Without this the system refuses to start one and says
	     nothing about why. See ios/Runner/BurnActivity.swift for what it
	     carries, which is a deadline and a count and no more: it is drawn on a
	     locked screen, which is the most public surface this phone has. -->
	<!-- The scheme the widget opens. `rotelyx://meet` goes straight to the
	     pairing screen with a code showing, which is what the home screen
	     widget is for. It carries nothing: a meeting code is minted when that
	     screen opens, never before, because one sitting in a URL is a meeting
	     anybody who saw the URL could attend. -->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>rotelyx</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSSupportsLiveActivities</key>
	<true/>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>Rotelyx Chat</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<!-- Export compliance.

	     TRUE, and this is not the cautious answer, it is the correct one.

	     The exemption most applications claim covers software that only calls
	     the encryption already in the operating system. This one does not: it
	     ships its own implementation of MLS and X-Wing in a Rust library, which
	     is exactly what the exemption excludes.

	     Declaring false here to skip the questionnaire is a false statement to
	     Apple and, through them, to the United States Bureau of Industry and
	     Security. What it actually requires is an annual self-classification
	     report, or a CCATS, before distribution. See docs/RELEASING.md, which
	     also covers the separate French declaration.
	-->
	<!-- Woken by a push, and by nothing else.

	     `remote-notification` is what lets the notification service extension
	     run when a content-free push arrives. There is no `fetch` and no
	     `processing`: this application does not poll in the background, because
	     iOS does not permit the socket that would make polling useful.

	     Notably absent: `audio` and `voip`. PushKit is the right mechanism for
	     an incoming call and the wrong one for a message, and Apple revokes the
	     entitlement of applications that use it for messages.
	-->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>remote-notification</string>
		<!-- Without this a call ends the moment the screen locks, which is when
		     most calls are actually held. `CallAudio.swift` says the session
		     category keeps the call alive through a lock; the category is
		     necessary and this is the other half, and the comment was written
		     on a machine with no way to try it.

		     Declared honestly: this application really does play and capture
		     audio while it is not in front, and only during a call. There is no
		     background playback of anything else, which is the use App Review
		     looks for when this key is present. -->
		<string>audio</string>
	</array>
	<!-- What iOS shows the person when it asks.

	     These are not boilerplate. iOS refuses to launch an application that
	     touches the microphone or camera without them, and the text is what the
	     person reads while deciding, so it says what the thing is for and stops.

	     Notably absent: NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription, which is the one that
	     grants read access to the whole photo library. This application has no
	     use for it and does not ask.

	     Choosing a photograph to send asks for nothing either. That goes
	     through PHPickerViewController, which runs outside this process and
	     hands back the one picture that was tapped; the library is never
	     visible to this application. See ios/Runner/FilePicker.swift.

	     What is present is NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription, the add-only
	     permission, and it is used at one moment: somebody presses save on a
	     picture a friend sent them. Add-only cannot list, open or count
	     anything in the library. It can put one picture in. See
	     ios/Runner/SaveToPhotos.swift.
	-->
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Rotelyx needs the microphone for voice calls. It is used only while a call is running.</string>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Rotelyx needs the camera to scan a meeting code. Nothing is recorded and nothing is sent.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Rotelyx asks for this only when you tap save on a picture someone sent you, so that it can put that one picture in your library. This is the add-only permission: Rotelyx cannot see, open or list anything already in your photos, and it never asks to. Choosing a photograph to send does not use this and asks for no permission at all.</string>
	<!-- ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption is deliberately absent, and absent is not
	     the same as false.

	     This application uses non-exempt encryption: it ships its own MLS and
	     X-Wing implementation rather than calling the operating system's, and
	     declaring false would be a false statement to Apple and, through them,
	     to the United States Bureau of Industry and Security. It is never to be
	     set false here.

	     Declaring true is the other half, and it requires the binary to carry
	     ITSEncryptionExportComplianceCode, the code App Store Connect issues
	     against uploaded encryption documentation. Without a matching code the
	     upload is refused at validation with "the export compliance key value
	     [] doesn't match", which is what happened.

	     With the key absent, App Store Connect asks the export questions per
	     submission instead and they are answered there, truthfully. To go back
	     to declaring it in the binary, put both keys in:

	         <key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key><true/>
	         <key>ITSEncryptionExportComplianceCode</key><string>THE-CODE</string>

	     docs/EXPORT-COMPLIANCE.md holds the answers either path needs.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(FLUTTER_BUILD_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleSignature</key>
	<string>????</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(FLUTTER_BUILD_NUMBER)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIMainStoryboardFile</key>
	<string>Main</string>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIApplicationSupportsIndirectInputEvents</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
